3.4 Coverage of RFC 0170 integration surfaces
---------------------------------------------

RFC 0170 is an integration specification; its cookbook roster is not the selection rule for this evaluation. AP2 (Appendix F) and s-you (Appendix G) remain main-evaluation candidates. The traditional interfaces in Appendices A–E move to this RFC's Appendix D, together with Apple Pay, Google Pay and the selected eBay marketplace interface. Appendix D.1 preserves the full crosswalk.

UCP, the §4 substrate of RFC 0170, is also positioned for agent commerce and is evaluated here on that basis. Mastercard VI/Agent Pay for Machines, Visa TAP and PayPal Agent Ready/Store Sync remain specifically identified agent-commerce offerings, not proxies for their entire companies. The distinction is offering-specific: Stripe PaymentIntents is a traditional substrate; Stripe's MPP integration is an agent-facing composition assessed under MPP.

CCPE-F candidacy does not establish conformance. A cookbook describes an integration, not evidence that the native vendor already implements GAuth or that the composition closes a candidate's C1–C9 gaps.

4. AP2 reference-case deep dive
===============================

4.1 Why AP2 is the reference case
---------------------------------

AP2 exposes sufficiently detailed consent, delegation, transaction-binding and verifier roles to exercise the common framework. It is used in the proven methodological role as in RFC 0130: a detailed case through which readers can apply and challenge the criteria. This is not a claim that AP2 represents every payment mechanism, and no AP2-specific finding is inherited by another solution without its own evidence.

4.2 Reviewed specification boundary
-----------------------------------

The assessment uses the four AP2 documents A1–A4 at commit e1ea56db72a6385bce3e5c1112b3a56ce60acb43. A recorded byte comparison against the then-resolvable v0.2.0 tag, commit b4587ac1d055888a73b4b21750973cffba961793, found those four files identical. This is an edition-control observation, not authentication of historical tag immutability, first disclosure or patent priority.

4.3 Capabilities and the independent-authority question
-------------------------------------------------------

The reviewed edition defines Checkout and Payment Mandates, open/closed forms, explicit user consent, key-bound delegation, transaction constraints and cumulative recurring budgets. Role-specific verification and signed linkage are substantive protections. They must not be reduced to authentication-only or described as absent merely because they use a different schema.

The separate question is whether principal-origin authority has independently verifiable evidence, a non-overridable decision and a current lifecycle distinct from payment/platform permission. Additional consent, delegation or budget features do not by themselves establish that separation. Historical corrections will be recorded in Appendix C rather than used as the organizing narrative.

4.4 User Credential model
-------------------------

The verifier trusts an issuer external to the agent to guarantee a Trusted Surface. The user approves mandate content; the delegation payload is included in key binding. The agent proves possession when binding an open mandate to a transaction. An issuer credential, user-approved delegation and agent action are distinguishable signed components. [A2]

This is stronger than a platform attaching unverifiable PoA-looking fields to an identity token. It directly weakens the universal “one merged credential/one verifier” premise. However, the reviewed documents do not require evidence of corporate representative capacity, current state of every upstream organizational mandate, independent authority revocation handles with cascade bounds, or two separately recorded authority/commerce verdicts.

Verdict: Principal-delegation architecture with partial separation; blanket S3 not established; complete S2 architectural properties not established by the reviewed evidence. To resolve a deployment, inspect actual issuer/holder trust, delegation signatures, both decision records and lifecycle state.

4.5 Trusted Agent Provider model
--------------------------------

The provider's deterministic Trusted Surface obtains consent; the provider signs the mandate using a key the agent must not access directly. Verifiers trust the provider. AP2 expressly permits the same entity to occupy multiple roles, but requires it to perform all corresponding responsibilities. [A1,A2]

This increases dependence on provider attestations. It does not prove that the provider is the merchant, processor or principal, nor that all decisions are collapsed. Where provider-signed payment mandates replace independently evaluated principal authority within the same authorization/lifecycle model, that deployment exhibits the S3 pattern. To confirm that diagnosis, show the merged authorization/lifecycle object and the absence of an independent authority decision in the assessed implementation—not just the provider's name.

Consequences of the S3 pattern. Where principal authority and payment permission are collapsed into the same authorization and lifecycle model, payment-credential validity is not an independent assurance that the underlying delegation remains current. Changes to authority may therefore leave previously issued credentials usable, while withdrawal of authority cannot be managed independently of the payment credential’s lifecycle. Cryptographic signatures and transaction constraints alone do not resolve this separation problem. GiFo-RFC 0170 §2.7 discusses these structural disadvantages. They apply where the S3 conditions above are established.

4.6 Seven architectural questions
---------------------------------

1. Mutation drift: AP2 constrains action content, but current upstream authority-state checks are not fully prescribed. Retain the freshness gap, not “unconstrained agent.”

2. Delegation event: Explicit consent/delegation exists. Evaluate its exact binding and assurance.

3. Parent chain: AP2 has verifiable open/closed chains and checkout/payment linkage. General multi-agent, multi-hop authority ancestry is a distinct test.

4. Revocation: Missing timed cascade is a legitimate specification-level distinction. Architectural impossibility is not demonstrated.

5. Interoperability: External User Credentials and provider-trust models differ. No universal vendor lock-in conclusion.

6. Governance versus format: Retain this distinction in both directions: signing more objects does not prove S2; co-carriage does not prove S3.

7. Immutability: Hashes, key binding and unchanged-claim checks are real integrity controls. They do not evidence fresh authority-state evaluation; assess that mechanism separately.

4.7 Decision and architecture-evidence checklist
------------------------------------------------

The reviewed AP2 specification does not establish the complete independent principal-authority and lifecycle guarantees required for S2 conformance. Where an AP2 deployment treats its payment mandates and associated checks as a substitute for that independent authority layer, it falls within the S3 pattern. Additional consent, delegation or budget features do not, by themselves, change that classification.

The User Credential model provides stronger evidence of the user’s authorization, but does not resolve the lifecycle gaps identified here: the reviewed architecture does not establish bounded ancestor-state freshness or independent cascading authority revocation. These gaps remain in both AP2 authorization models.

Assessment. The reviewed AP2 architecture does not establish the independent authority and lifecycle controls required for S2. It exhibits S3 characteristics where provider-signed payment mandates substitute for independently evaluated principal authority within the same authorization and lifecycle model. The User Credential model strengthens evidence of user authorization but does not resolve the identified ancestor-freshness and cascading-revocation gaps. It therefore does not change the S2 assessment.

5. Other mechanisms: assessment and closure needs
=================================================

ACP: The reviewed specification introduction defines deterministic checkout and payment-token relay while retaining seller systems. It does not establish a portable PoA chain. A native checkout-only deployment is S1-shaped at that boundary; the protocol can be composed with other authority controls. Inspect delegated-payment constraints and actual agent consent/issuance records before extending that finding. [B1]

UCP: A commerce substrate with capabilities/extensions and AP2 support. Do not classify discovery or checkout transport as S3. Inspect which authority extension is negotiated and how its decision gates checkout completion. [B2]

x402: Facilitator verification and settlement support paid resource access. A successful payment does not identify the human who authorized the agent's task or prove representative capacity. Inspect the buyer wallet's delegation/policy layer separately; no protocol-wide S3 verdict follows. [B3] MPP: The reviewed core includes payment challenges, request binding, receipts, replay protection and idempotency. Those are meaningful controls. Its payment credential is not automatically a general PoA credential. Inspect wallet authority before payment and distinguish a protocol receipt from a complete delegation audit. “MPP” here means Machine Payments Protocol, not AP2's Merchant Payment Processor role. [B4]

Visa TAP: Signed HTTP requests help recognize approved agents and protect request integrity. Consumer/payment signature surfaces merit inspection, not an assumption of identity-only capability. The overview does not establish all C1–C9 properties. Closure needs the exact consumer-consent and payment containers, signer roles and revocation behavior for the chosen onboarding model. [B5]

Mastercard VI: L1 credential, L2 user intent and L3 agent fulfilment form a meaningful cryptographic chain. The v0.1 text distinguishes immediate/autonomous modes and payment-network state for cumulative constraints. It also explicitly defers VI-layer credential revocation. It therefore provides substantive delegation evidence but not the complete G-Commerce lifecycle contract. Neither layer count nor missing revocation alone proves S3. Closure needs network enforcement traces and a defined authority-revocation design. [B6]

KYAPay: The model explicitly distinguishes identity-only KYA, payment-only Pay and combined KYAPay tokens. Identity/payment combination is not identical to PoA/payment collapse. Its sub, buyer identity and amount fields do not establish the entire C1–C9 chain. Inspect token issuance, delegated authority, lifecycle and merchant verification; do not claim full v1.0 maturity when the README says forthcoming. [B7]

APP: The OKX overview describes wallet/session-key infrastructure, HTTP 402 and payment modes. Distinguish advertised open protocol from available SDK functions; escrow is explicitly forthcoming in part of the same page. An upstream principal authority and revocation contract remains U here. Closure requires versioned protocol/security text and actual wallet enforcement, not marketing comparisons. [B8]

Network/provider offerings: Mastercard Machines, PayPal Agent Ready and Nevermined should remain in the inventory because they can supply relevant controls. Their product evidence does not justify an automatic S2/S3 decision. Inspect enrolled agent/owner attribution, spend delegation, enforcement ownership, revocation and persisted receipts for the specific service configuration. [B9–B11]

5.1 s-you / EUDIPal Scoped Mandate
----------------------------------

Established documentary scope — contract v2. The interface dated 17 October describes one bank-held mandate with two parts: payment scope in the clear (currency, transaction/total limits, validity and merchant restrictions), and a user-signed checkout part (goods categories and exclusions) stored sealed at the bank. The wallet displays both parts and checks their hash linkage before approval. The merchant verifies the checkout signature and goods restrictions; the bank receives the full merchant attestation and verifies its signature, mandate, checkout hash, merchant and amount, plus freshness and unused attestation identity. Payment orders remain signed by the agent's separate transport key. Direct mandate revocation and checkout-status lookup are documented. Bank and merchant remain mocks; settlement status is simulated, with no money movement. This is not evidence of a production Sparkasse rollout. [S2 §§1.2–1.14, 2.3]

Consent and trust paths. Local device authentication with posted hash/method claims remains path (a). Detailed §1.14 documents path (b), the bank as OpenID4VP/PaSO verifier: signed request, credential/key binding, audience and nonce checks, transaction-data hash verification and consent claims. Its hash covers the transmitted transaction-data entry, unlike path (a)'s canonical JSON hash. Section 1.15 additionally documents bank-issued SCA credentials through OpenID4VCI with PAR/PKCE, an authenticated mock banking session, configured issuer anchors and credential-status checks. The mock bank and demo ecosystem accept specified credential types under documented trust rules. These are substantive documented capabilities, not merely future aspirations. Wallet-side acceptance without configured roots and availability of the weaker path still require explicit production policy. Operator-issued EBW credentials remain a target; operator identity is optional under the v2 bank policy. [S2 §§1.9–1.15, 3]

Edition and implementation limits. The interface says v2 is implemented in work packages R2–R5, but warns that live builds ≤759 may retain v1 behavior. Its overview/Phase-2 table, the public overview and parts of the target page still place PaSO authorization or signed scope in the future. This review uses the detailed v2 contract for documentary requirements, records that inconsistency and does not infer which build is running. Neither the source's test claims nor deployment security were independently verified. Pin the build, authentication path, issuer/wallet trust configuration and machine-readable contract before operational reliance. The native source's “Phase 1/Phase 2” roadmap labels are not the CCPE enforcement phases. [S1–S3]

