A.6 What must exist before implementation claims
------------------------------------------------

Cooperating AP2 verifiers and UCP endpoint; chosen credential/trust model; verified representative-capacity process; agreed canonical action binding; Power-PEP checks; persistent atomic ledger; revocation/freshness SLA; pre-dispatch target enforcement; receipt verification; protected audit store; failure reconciliation and adversarial tests. No adapter has been built or certified by this document.

Card/token paths do not remove normal payment compliance obligations. x402, stablecoin or other Web3 variants are not silently covered by an open CCPE-F conformance claim. Review licence scope and excluded settlement semantics separately.

A.7 Berlin–Singapore worked boundary case
-----------------------------------------

This illustrative case uses a hypothetical EUR 50,000 corporate purchasing mandate. A concrete person acting in verified representative capacity for a Berlin company delegates purchasing authority to an agent dealing with a Singapore supplier. For this example, assume an aggregate EUR 50,000 ceiling, no other applicable lower limit and no earlier reservations. These are explicit assumptions: an actual mandate must state whether its cap is per order, per period or cumulative, and whether taxes, shipping and conversion costs are included.

Before the supplier accepts an order—not merely before payment—the parties need an agreed issuer/capacity trust basis and a common interpretation of the mandate. The supplier's acceptance path must obtain and verify transaction-bound authority evidence, including current status and the applicable decision, rather than trust an unbound claim from the buyer's agent. A shared budget requires trustworthy reservation evidence, not disclosure of unrelated purchases. If the order is denominated in SGD, agree the EUR conversion source, time, rounding and reservation buffer; do not compare unlike currency amounts.

