D.3 Payment methods and marketplace interface
---------------------------------------------

Apple Pay EC_v1 [P6]. Apple-root certificate-chain and signature checks authenticate encrypted payment data, transaction ID and optional application-data hash. The merchant selects its decryption key through publicKeyHash, checks amount/currency against the original request and checks optional order/application binding. Signing-time comparison and rejection of processed transaction IDs address payment replay. These are native token-verification controls. An authority composition must link the verified payment to the independently authorized action; device/card authentication is not corporate representative-capacity evidence.

Google Pay ECv2 [P7]. Google root and intermediate-key verification authenticate a signed encrypted payload. Signature input binds recipient_id to merchant:merchantId; integrators check key/message expiration and decrypt card or tokenized-card data. Message ID identifies the payload but is not, by itself, a durable deduplication or shared-budget protocol. The reviewed encrypted-message schema does not bind an order amount or agent grant. An integration must supply that authenticated action linkage rather than borrowing Apple Pay's different field semantics. This payment-method interface is not another AP2 variant.

eBay Buy Order v1 member checkout [P8]. The selected marketplace overlay combines buyer OAuth, an app/marketplace-bound checkout session and an order/payment transition. placeOrder requires an authorization-code token with buy.order, checkout-session ID and matching marketplace header. It creates the order and terminates the session. Warning 15006 identifies an already placed order; error 15020 requires a new session after an item change. Order ID is explicitly distinct from payment success. An added authority layer must guard this executing order boundary, bind the exact checkout and preserve status/recovery evidence. The member path is assessed, not a conflation with guest checkout or every marketplace API.

D.4 Composition safeguards
--------------------------

RFC 0170 §§2.5/3.1.3 require pre-execution authority and permission, authenticated action binding, reservation/consumption/dispatch ordering and safe recovery. A payment response cannot retroactively supply the permission that should have preceded it. Native calls that create holds or other financial effects are not read-only preflight checks.

Any proposed binding must identify the protected commitment point, selected native statuses, idempotency retention/equality rules, cancellation limitations and recovery path. Authority invalidation before handoff must block the effect; after an irreversible handoff, revocation is not automatic rollback. An interface lacking the required enforcement seam is not made conformant merely by being a candidate.

These are integration requirements, not additional coverage credited to native candidates in the main evaluation. They preserve RFC 0170's broad integration purpose while RFC 0171 evaluates agent-commerce solutions against common architectural requirements.

